<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<mutations>
  <!--
        The first mutation this fork declares. Every <mutation> needs a Class, and a Class is a
        C# type the game resolves as "XRL.World.Parts.Mutation." + Class - so a mutation cannot
        be added in XML alone. See mod/Scripting/Vixy_Fangs.cs and issue #589.

        Class must NOT be a vanilla mutation's class. Two entries sharing one class collide:
        MutationFactory.Init sorts each category's entries by display name and then builds
        _MutationsByClass from that order, and BaseMutation.GetMutationEntry separates entries
        sharing a class by exact Variant match OR "this entry has variants and the pool contains
        this one" - and that second test is class-wide, so it is true for every entry and index 0
        always wins. Whichever entry sorts first swallows the other, vanilla's included. This is
        the same failure as Akimbo in #11, and docs/LESSONS.md records it.

        Variant names the natural weapon blueprint. RegrowHorns-style code reads it as a blueprint
        name, so it must match an <object Name=> in ObjectBlueprints - here Vixy_Fangs, in
        MeleeWeapons.xml, which carries the MutationEquipment tag naming this class back.

        Category Name="Physical" merges into vanilla's category rather than declaring a new one:
        HandleCategoryNode looks the name up and reuses the existing MutationCategory when it
        finds it. Filing under Physical also inherits the Chimera and Esper exclusions for free -
        a new category is excluded by neither, so an Esper could take this. See #471.
    -->

  <category Name="Physical">
    <mutation
      Name="Fangs"
      Cost="3"
      MaxSelected="1"
      Class="Vixy_Fangs"
      Variant="Vixy_Fangs"
      Exclusions="Beak"
      BearerDescription="the fanged"
      Tile="Items/girshling_fangs.bmp"
    />

    <!--
        Cost 1, and that is why it does not level. Every 1-point physical mutation vanilla ships
        returns false from CanLevel and an empty string from GetLevelText - Beak, Thick Fur, Night
        Vision, Slime Glands - so a point buys flat effects, permanently. The footing save scales off
        the character's Agility instead, which is the only growth a mutation at this price can have.

        Variant names the default tail; the other four are picked at chargen. Every variant behaves
        identically - they exist so a player can say which animal they are, which is a roleplaying
        choice rather than a mechanical one. BaseMutation.SelectVariant renders each blueprint's own
        name and tile in the picker.

        No Exclusions. The tail occupies the Tail part, which nothing else in this fork touches, and
        vanilla's own Stinger claims the same part through a manager id rather than by owning it - so
        a character may take both and Stinger will find its own.
    -->

    <mutation
      Name="Tail"
      Cost="1"
      MaxSelected="1"
      Class="Vixy_Tail"
      Variant="Vixy_FoxTail"
      Exclusions=""
      BearerDescription="the tailed"
      Tile="Creatures/natural-weapon-tail.bmp"
    />
  </category>
</mutations>
